Authorization application and drug prescription review:
The insurance money applicant submits an authorization application for purchasing specific drugs at a designated malignant tumor pharmacy to the insurer (hereinafter referred to as "authorization application"), and provides the following authorization application materials:
1. Insurance money payment application form;
2. Valid identity documents of the insured;
3. All certificates, information, and evidence supporting the review, including but not limited to outpatient and inpatient medical records, medical diagnosis certificates, pathological examination reports, imaging reports, inspection and test reports, original medical expense documents, original expense breakdown documents, etc. issued by hospitals or medical institutions. If the insurance money applicant cannot provide the above materials for special reasons, other legal and effective materials capable of proving the insurance accident shall be provided;
4. Specific drug prescriptions issued by specialist doctors in hospitals or medical institutions;
5. External purchase drug certificates issued by hospitals or medical institutions;
6. Other certificates and materials related to confirming the nature, cause, and degree of loss of the insurance accident that can be provided by the insurance money applicant.
The insurer conducts drug prescription review based on the submitted materials. If the relevant materials submitted by the insurance money applicant are insufficient to support the drug prescription review, or the results of scientific inspection reports related to the medical materials do not support the issuance of the drug prescription, the insurer has the right to require and notify the insurance money applicant to provide supplementary relevant certificates and materials at one time.
If the insurance money applicant fails to submit the authorization application or the prescription review fails, the insurer shall not assume the responsibility of paying insurance money for malignant tumor specific drug expenses.

